WEBVTT

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:06.847
This is how you build a real app without
writing the code yourself.

00:00:07.397 --> 00:00:10.580
You describe what you want in plain
English.

00:00:10.580 --> 00:00:13.980
An agent writes it, on GitHub, while you
watch.

00:00:14.530 --> 00:00:20.143
This is called vibe coding, and this video
walks the whole loop, step by step.

00:00:20.693 --> 00:00:25.568
By the end you will have a repository, a
pull request, and a working site.

00:00:27.368 --> 00:00:31.326
Vibe coding means you work in intention,
not in syntax.

00:00:31.876 --> 00:00:36.762
You say what you want. You look at the
result. You say what to change.

00:00:37.312 --> 00:00:42.550
The agent writes the files, runs the
tools, and records the work as commits.

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:45.372
That does not mean you stop thinking.

00:00:45.922 --> 00:00:49.772
It means your attention goes on the
decision, not on the typing.

00:00:50.322 --> 00:00:52.739
Three rules carry the rest of this video.

00:00:53.289 --> 00:00:56.877
One. Small steps, one change at a time.

00:00:57.427 --> 00:01:00.361
Two. Read the diff before you accept it.

00:01:00.911 --> 00:01:05.502
Three. Keep every change in Git, so any
step can be undone.

00:01:07.302 --> 00:01:09.131
Here is the loop we will repeat.

00:01:09.681 --> 00:01:14.556
You write a prompt. The agent plans the
work, and edits a branch.

00:01:15.106 --> 00:01:19.028
GitHub records the change, and the checks
run automatically.

00:01:19.578 --> 00:01:23.077
You read the pull request, leave a note,
and merge.

00:01:23.627 --> 00:01:26.746
Then you go again, one small step further.

00:01:27.296 --> 00:01:33.005
Six parts. Prompt, plan, branch, diff,
checks, merge.

00:01:33.555 --> 00:01:36.697
Every step in this video is one turn of
that loop.

00:01:38.497 --> 00:01:43.040
Step one. A GitHub account, and one empty
repository.

00:01:43.590 --> 00:01:48.230
Go to github.com, sign up, and confirm
your email address.

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:52.332
Then press New repository, in the top
right corner.

00:01:52.882 --> 00:01:57.574
Give it a short name, in lower case, with
hyphens between the words.

00:01:58.124 --> 00:02:01.979
Choose Public if you want free checks and
a public site.

00:02:02.529 --> 00:02:05.758
Choose Private if the work is not ready to
be seen.

00:02:06.308 --> 00:02:12.214
Tick Add a README. It gives the repository
its first file, and its first commit.

00:02:12.764 --> 00:02:17.245
Press Create repository, and you are
looking at the home of your project.

00:02:17.795 --> 00:02:20.955
That page has four parts you will use
every day.

00:02:21.505 --> 00:02:27.087
The file list. The commit history. The
branches. And the Actions tab.

00:02:27.637 --> 00:02:32.194
The Actions tab is where the checks run.
We will come back to it.

00:02:32.744 --> 00:02:37.012
That is all the Git you need for now. The
rest is conversation.

00:02:38.812 --> 00:02:43.346
Step two. Connect GitHub to Arena. You do
this once.

00:02:43.896 --> 00:02:48.207
In Arena, open the GitHub connection, and
approve the sign in.

00:02:48.757 --> 00:02:52.159
Then choose which repositories the agent
may touch.

00:02:52.709 --> 00:02:55.493
Start with one. You can add more later.

00:02:56.043 --> 00:02:58.340
Read that permission screen properly.

00:02:58.890 --> 00:03:03.032
It is the difference between a useful
helper and a very fast hazard.

00:03:03.582 --> 00:03:07.626
Now open your repository in Arena, and
start a conversation.

00:03:08.176 --> 00:03:13.155
The agent can see the files, the history,
and the branch it is working on.

00:03:13.705 --> 00:03:17.374
Your repository name sits at the top of
the conversation.

00:03:17.924 --> 00:03:19.902
Check it every single time.

00:03:19.902 --> 00:03:23.712
Wrong repository is the most expensive
easy mistake.

00:03:25.512 --> 00:03:31.017
Step three. The first prompt. This is
where most people lose the thread.

00:03:31.567 --> 00:03:34.307
A weak prompt says, make me an app.

00:03:34.857 --> 00:03:39.934
A strong prompt says what it is, who it is
for, and what done looks like.

00:03:40.484 --> 00:03:46.837
It names the pages. It names the files, if
you care. It names what is out of scope.

00:03:47.387 --> 00:03:49.365
Here is a prompt that works.

00:03:49.915 --> 00:03:53.352
Build a one page site that lists my
favourite books.

00:03:53.902 --> 00:03:59.338
It should read a file called books dot
json, and draw one card for each book.

00:03:59.888 --> 00:04:06.879
Keep it to plain HTML, CSS and JavaScript.
No framework, and no build step.

00:04:07.429 --> 00:04:09.915
Add a README that explains how to run it.

00:04:10.465 --> 00:04:12.145
Do not add a database yet.

00:04:12.695 --> 00:04:18.014
That last sentence matters. It stops the
agent building three weeks of work.

00:04:18.564 --> 00:04:22.237
Send it, then read the plan before it
writes anything.

00:04:22.787 --> 00:04:27.595
If the plan is wrong, say so now, while it
is still cheap to change.

00:04:29.395 --> 00:04:34.248
Step four. The agent works in a branch,
not on your main files.

00:04:34.798 --> 00:04:39.224
A branch is a copy of the project where
changes can be tried safely.

00:04:39.774 --> 00:04:44.829
You will see the branch name in the pull
request, something like feature slash book

00:04:44.829 --> 00:04:45.134
list.

00:04:45.684 --> 00:04:51.291
Behind the scenes, it reads files, writes
files, and runs what it can run.

00:04:51.841 --> 00:04:56.466
Each of those steps appears in the
conversation, as a line you can expand.

00:04:57.016 --> 00:05:02.024
Read those lines. They say what actually
happened, not what was promised.

00:05:02.574 --> 00:05:07.397
When the work is finished, it opens a pull
request and links it in the chat.

00:05:07.947 --> 00:05:11.415
Nothing is live yet. That is exactly the
point.

00:05:13.215 --> 00:05:17.677
Step five. Read the diff. This is the
skill worth learning.

00:05:18.227 --> 00:05:22.807
A diff shows every line added in green,
and every line removed in red.

00:05:23.357 --> 00:05:28.507
Start with the file list. If a file
changed that should not have, stop.

00:05:29.057 --> 00:05:33.763
Then read the new file from the top. It is
usually shorter than you fear.

00:05:34.313 --> 00:05:36.393
Look for the three usual problems.

00:05:36.943 --> 00:05:40.392
One. It added a dependency you did not ask
for.

00:05:40.942 --> 00:05:44.828
Two. It hard coded a value that should
have been a setting.

00:05:45.378 --> 00:05:48.883
Three. It wrote a test that tests nothing
at all.

00:05:49.433 --> 00:05:54.308
If something is wrong, do not fix it by
hand. Ask for the change.

00:05:54.858 --> 00:05:59.059
One clear sentence in the conversation is
worth ten manual edits.

00:06:00.859 --> 00:06:03.520
Step six. Let the checks run.

00:06:04.070 --> 00:06:09.409
Every pull request starts GitHub Actions.
Those are the checks at the bottom.

00:06:09.959 --> 00:06:12.673
Green means the build and the tests
passed.

00:06:13.223 --> 00:06:17.103
Red means something failed, and the log
will tell you what.

00:06:17.653 --> 00:06:22.299
Sometimes a check is red because of the
setup, not because of your code.

00:06:22.849 --> 00:06:27.717
Read the first error only. Fixing the
first error often fixes the rest.

00:06:28.267 --> 00:06:32.316
Ask the agent to fix it, and it pushes to
the same branch.

00:06:32.866 --> 00:06:37.365
The pull request updates itself. You do
not open a second one.

00:06:37.915 --> 00:06:39.912
Wait for green before you merge.

00:06:40.462 --> 00:06:44.416
Not because rules are nice, but because
green is information.

00:06:46.216 --> 00:06:49.566
Step seven. Merge, and put it online.

00:06:50.116 --> 00:06:53.117
Press Merge pull request, then Confirm.

00:06:53.667 --> 00:06:58.400
The branch is folded into main, and the
pull request closes itself.

00:06:58.950 --> 00:07:03.063
If you turned on GitHub Pages, main is now
a live website.

00:07:03.613 --> 00:07:08.624
The address is your username.github.io,
slash the repository name.

00:07:09.174 --> 00:07:12.805
It takes about a minute. Refresh until it
appears.

00:07:13.355 --> 00:07:18.006
Your first version is live, and every
change after this is the same loop.

00:07:18.556 --> 00:07:23.391
Small prompt. Read the diff. Wait for
green. Merge.

00:07:23.941 --> 00:07:25.296
That is the whole job.

00:07:27.096 --> 00:07:31.617
Let us do it once more, in real time, with
a second feature.

00:07:32.167 --> 00:07:35.841
The site lists books. Now you want a
search box.

00:07:36.391 --> 00:07:41.393
The prompt names the outcome, the
constraint, and the thing to leave alone.

00:07:41.943 --> 00:07:43.985
Add a search box above the list.

00:07:44.535 --> 00:07:49.787
It should filter the cards as I type,
ignore capital letters, and show a message

00:07:49.787 --> 00:07:51.166
when nothing matches.

00:07:51.716 --> 00:07:55.571
Do not change the card design, and do not
add a library.

00:07:56.121 --> 00:08:01.059
One file changes. One pull request. About
forty lines.

00:08:01.609 --> 00:08:04.454
You read the diff in under a minute, and
you merge.

00:08:05.004 --> 00:08:08.717
That is what ten thousand commits look
like from the outside.

00:08:09.267 --> 00:08:14.238
Small, reviewed, reversible steps, stacked
until it is a product.

00:08:16.038 --> 00:08:18.940
Step eight. What to do when it goes wrong.

00:08:19.490 --> 00:08:25.177
It will go wrong. The tool is fast, and
fast tools make confident mistakes.

00:08:25.727 --> 00:08:30.799
If the site breaks, open the pull request
that broke it, and press Revert.

00:08:31.349 --> 00:08:36.107
That creates a new commit which undoes the
old one. Nothing is lost.

00:08:36.657 --> 00:08:41.304
If the agent goes in circles, start a new
conversation with a smaller ask.

00:08:41.854 --> 00:08:46.202
A long confused conversation gets more
confused, not less.

00:08:46.752 --> 00:08:50.720
If it deleted something you wanted, the
history still has it.

00:08:51.270 --> 00:08:53.203
That is the whole reason we commit.

00:08:53.753 --> 00:08:57.287
Recovery is a skill. Practise it before
you need it.

00:08:59.087 --> 00:09:02.845
Step nine. The patterns that make prompts
work.

00:09:03.395 --> 00:09:05.778
Name the outcome, not the technique.

00:09:06.328 --> 00:09:09.798
Say what the user sees, not which function
to write.

00:09:10.348 --> 00:09:14.883
Give one example. A rough example beats a
perfect adjective.

00:09:15.433 --> 00:09:21.754
State the constraint. No framework. Under
fifty lines. Do not touch the header.

00:09:22.304 --> 00:09:25.085
Ask for a plan first when the task is big.

00:09:25.635 --> 00:09:29.951
Say what to leave alone. That sentence
saves the most time.

00:09:30.501 --> 00:09:35.288
Ask for one idea per pull request, because
one idea is easy to review.

00:09:35.838 --> 00:09:38.640
Paste the error, not your theory about the
error.

00:09:39.190 --> 00:09:43.788
And when it works, say so, and ask for the
next smallest step.

00:09:44.338 --> 00:09:46.843
You are steering. It is not driving.

00:09:48.643 --> 00:09:52.526
Step ten. Keep your secrets out of the
repository.

00:09:53.076 --> 00:09:57.025
Never paste a password, a key, or a token
into a file.

00:09:57.575 --> 00:10:03.071
If a project needs a secret, put it in the
repository settings, under Secrets.

00:10:03.621 --> 00:10:06.290
A public repository is public forever.

00:10:06.840 --> 00:10:10.006
Assume that anything committed will
eventually be read.

00:10:10.556 --> 00:10:15.226
If a key does leak, revoke it first, and
clean the history second.

00:10:15.776 --> 00:10:20.655
Revoking takes ten seconds. Cleaning
history takes an afternoon.

00:10:22.455 --> 00:10:25.921
Step eleven. The habits that keep this
fast.

00:10:26.471 --> 00:10:31.663
Keep main working. Break work in branches,
and merge only what is green.

00:10:32.213 --> 00:10:34.512
One pull request, one idea.

00:10:35.062 --> 00:10:39.809
Reviewing ten small ones beats reviewing
one huge one, every time.

00:10:40.359 --> 00:10:44.639
Write the README as you go, so the next
prompt has context.

00:10:45.189 --> 00:10:49.268
Write commit messages in plain English.
Future you is a reader.

00:10:49.818 --> 00:10:54.380
Keep the list of what is next inside the
repository, not in your head.

00:10:54.930 --> 00:10:57.599
And once a week, look at what you shipped.

00:10:58.149 --> 00:11:00.538
The list is usually longer than you think.

00:11:01.088 --> 00:11:04.214
Speed comes from the loop, not from the
typing.

00:11:06.014 --> 00:11:08.625
Let us put the whole loop back together.

00:11:09.175 --> 00:11:14.660
Create the repository. Connect the
account. Check the repository name.

00:11:15.210 --> 00:11:19.375
Write a small prompt that names the
outcome and the constraint.

00:11:19.925 --> 00:11:23.872
Let the agent work in a branch, and read
what it says it did.

00:11:24.422 --> 00:11:28.280
Read the diff, file by file, and ask for
changes.

00:11:28.830 --> 00:11:33.352
Wait for green checks, then merge, then
watch the site update.

00:11:33.902 --> 00:11:37.455
When it breaks, revert, and take a smaller
step.

00:11:38.005 --> 00:11:41.005
That is vibe coding on Arena, through
GitHub.

00:11:42.805 --> 00:11:45.571
You do not need to know how to write every
line.

00:11:46.121 --> 00:11:49.302
You need to know what you want, and how to
check it.

00:11:49.852 --> 00:11:54.042
Start with one page, one prompt, one pull
request.

00:11:54.592 --> 00:11:58.979
Then do it again tomorrow, and let the
repository become the proof.
